Your small-ratio extension works. Your large-ratio exponent also works as an upper bound, but the shrinking prime scale is unnecessary, and the finite-\(H\) crossover needs correction. The curvature calculation gives a valid obstruction for an explicitly defined covering model; it does not give a general factoring lower bound.

I obtain no \(N^{1/6+o(1)}\) algorithm covering every band \(R\le N^\theta\) for fixed \(\theta>0\). There is, however, a useful extraction improvement: standard collision recovery, combined with the 2026 Harvey–Hittmeir order theorem and seven prime blocks, removes the order exception entirely.

Everything below is read-only. Write
\[
t=q/p,\qquad R\le t\le2R,\qquad
P=\sqrt{N/R},\qquad Q=\sqrt{NR}=RP.
\]
Thus \(p\in[P/\sqrt2,P]\). Put \(h=H^2\). Tildes suppress powers of \(\log N\); span identities are stated without that suppression. For pair-counting arguments, take the parameters from the bit-length scale \(X=2^n\), replacing \(N\) by \(X\) in their definitions.

**1. The rectangle works, and its actual span is substantially smaller than your proposed bound.**

Take
\[
V_R=\left\{(A,B):
1\le A\le\left\lfloor H/\sqrt R\right\rfloor,\quad
1\le B\le\left\lfloor H\sqrt R\right\rfloor,\quad
(A,B)=1\right\}.
\]
For \(R\le H^2\),
\[
|V_R|=\Theta(H^2)
\]
uniformly, including the endpoint where the numerator range has length one.

For \(F(x)=Bx+AN/x\), its exact minimum on \(I=[P/\sqrt2,P]\) is attained at
\[
x_*=\operatorname{clamp}\!\left(\sqrt{AN/B},I\right),
\]
and its maximum is attained at an endpoint. Therefore the exact real span is
\[
S_{A,B}
=\max\{F(P/\sqrt2),F(P)\}-F(x_*).
\]

In fact,
\[
\boxed{S_{A,B}\le(\sqrt2-1)H\sqrt N.}
\]
To prove it, let \(x<y\) belong to \(I\). Then
\[
F(x)-F(y)
\le AN(1/x-1/y)
\le(\sqrt2-1)AQ
\le(\sqrt2-1)H\sqrt N,
\]
whereas
\[
F(y)-F(x)
\le B(y-x)
\le(1-1/\sqrt2)BP
\le(1-1/\sqrt2)H\sqrt N.
\]
Taking the maximum over \(x,y\) proves the bound.

Thus your \((1+\sqrt2)H\sqrt N\) bound is certainly true. It also follows immediately from
\[
F(x)\le BP+\sqrt2\,AQ\le(1+\sqrt2)H\sqrt N.
\]
The stronger bound uses the actual interval minimum. Using the notes’ global AM–GM lower endpoint instead gives a larger, still valid window. Integer rounding adds at most two.

The residue classes remain distinct under the same modulus condition as in D′. Indeed,
\[
|AB'-A'B|<H^2,
\]
despite the larger upper bound for \(B\). Primitive distinct pairs therefore give distinct classes \(Aq/B\bmod\ell\) whenever \(\ell>h\).

Here is the general accounting used throughout. Suppose there are \(K\) weight/window specifications, each with span at most \(S\), and primes \(\ell\asymp Y\). Then
\[
T\le2K|\mathcal P|=O(KY/\log Y),
\qquad
W_\tau\le S/\ell^2+2.
\]
With baby length \(m\), the number of giants is exactly
\[
G=\sum_\tau\left\lceil W_\tau/m\right\rceil.
\]
The resulting bit-cost bound is
\[
\widetilde O\left(KY+m+\frac{KS}{mY}\right).
\]
The convenient endpoint choice
\[
m=\lceil S/Y^2\rceil+2
\]
gives
\[
\widetilde O(KY+S/Y^2).
\]
Balancing yields
\[
Y\asymp(S/K)^{1/3},\qquad
m\asymp K^{2/3}S^{1/3}.
\]

For the rectangle, \(K\asymp h\) and \(S\asymp H\sqrt N\), hence
\[
\boxed{
Y\asymp N^{1/6}H^{-1/3},\qquad
m\asymp H^{5/3}N^{1/6},\qquad
C_R=\widetilde O(H^{5/3}N^{1/6}).
}
\]
There is no power of \(R\).

For \(J\) dyadic ratio bands, the same primes, cofactor handles and baby list can be shared within each prime block. At the original \(Y\), only the targets acquire a factor \(J\). Reoptimizing jointly gives
\[
Y\asymp N^{1/6}H^{-1/3}J^{-1/3},
\qquad
C=\widetilde O(J^{2/3}H^{5/3}N^{1/6}).
\]
Either version has the same exponent when \(J=O(\log N)\).

Consequently, with D′’s \(H\asymp(\log N)N^{\varepsilon/2}\), ratios through \(N^\varepsilon\) cost nothing further in the exponent.

**2. Your large-ratio cost is correct, but there is a simpler construction with a growing prime scale.**

First, your fine-band calculation checks out. Partition \([R,2R]\) into intervals of width \(h\), and in each use \(A=1\) and \(h\) nearby consecutive values of \(B\). For \(t,B\) in the same interval,
\[
F(p)-2\sqrt{BN}
=\sqrt N\,\frac{(t-B)^2}
{\sqrt t\,(\sqrt t+\sqrt B)^2}
\le\frac{\sqrt N\,h^2}{4R^{3/2}}.
\]
There are \(\asymp R/h\) intervals and \(h\) weights per interval, so
\[
K\asymp R,\qquad
S\ll\sqrt N\,h^2R^{-3/2}.
\]
Therefore
\[
\boxed{
Y_{\rm fine}\asymp
H^{4/3}N^{1/6}R^{-5/6},\qquad
m\asymp RY_{\rm fine},\qquad
C_R=\widetilde O(H^{4/3}N^{1/6}R^{1/6}).
}
\]

But neither fine bands nor weights near \(R\) are necessary. For every \(R\ge h\), simply take
\[
\boxed{V_R=\{(1,B):1\le B\le h\}.}
\]
There are \(h\) primitive weights and \(h\) distinct classes at every \(\ell>h\).

Since \(B\le R\), \(F(x)=Bx+N/x\) is decreasing throughout \(I\). Its exact span is
\[
S_B
=(\sqrt2-1)Q-(1-1/\sqrt2)BP
\le(\sqrt2-1)Q.
\]
Thus \(K=h\), \(S\asymp Q\), and
\[
\boxed{
Y_{\rm coarse}\asymp(Q/h)^{1/3}
=N^{1/6}R^{1/6}H^{-2/3},
}
\]
\[
\boxed{
m\asymp h^{2/3}Q^{1/3}
=H^{4/3}N^{1/6}R^{1/6},
\qquad
C_R=\widetilde O(H^{4/3}N^{1/6}R^{1/6}).
}
\]
This attains your cost with \(h\) weight specifications instead of \(R\), and with primes whose size increases with \(R\). The arithmetic-progression construction across \([R,2R]\) also works, but consecutive small \(B\)’s already suffice.

The three constructions compare as follows:

| Construction | Specifications \(K\) | Span bound \(S\), up to constants | Optimized \(Y\) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Rectangle, \(R\le h\) | \(H^2\) | \(H\sqrt N\) | \(N^{1/6}H^{-1/3}\) |
| Fine bands, \(R>h\) | \(R\) | \(\sqrt N H^4R^{-3/2}\) | \(N^{1/6}H^{4/3}R^{-5/6}\) |
| Consecutive \(B\)’s, \(R>h\) | \(H^2\) | \(\sqrt{NR}\) | \(N^{1/6}R^{1/6}H^{-2/3}\) |

Your proposed cutoff and crossover are different quantities. In the fine-band parametrization,
\[
Y_{\rm fine}=H^2
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\boxed{R=N^{1/5}H^{-4/5}.}
\]
Pollard–Strassen costs \(\widetilde O(\sqrt P)=\widetilde O((N/R)^{1/4})\), by the standard small-factor bound. [Harvey, Proposition 2.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2010.05450)

Equating this with the DCL cost gives
\[
H^{4/3}N^{1/6}R^{1/6}=(N/R)^{1/4}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\boxed{R_*=N^{1/5}H^{-16/5}.}
\]
At that crossover,
\[
C_*=\widetilde O(N^{1/5}H^{4/5}),
\qquad
Y_{\rm fine}=H^4.
\]
Thus Pollard–Strassen becomes competitive before the fine construction reaches \(Y=H^2\). Both thresholds are \(N^{1/5+o(1)}\) when \(H=N^{o(1)}\).

There is also an extraction issue to address before calling these arithmetic budgets almost-all factoring theorems. Merely substituting larger weights into D′’s original FULL-count estimate is insufficient. Here is a rigorous replacement.

Harvey–Hittmeir’s June 2026 theorem deterministically finds \(\alpha\) with
\[
\operatorname{ord}_N(\alpha)>D
\]
or a proper divisor, for every \(1\le D<N-1\), in \(\widetilde O(\sqrt D)\) time. [Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2601.11131)

Apply it with \(D\ge m\). Use seven disjoint prime blocks, all primes exceeding \(Y\), with \(Y^7>N\). At least one block is coprime to
\[
(p-1)(q-1)<N:
\]
otherwise seven distinct primes exceeding \(Y\) would divide that product. For its exponent \(L\),
\[
\gcd(L,\operatorname{ord}_N(\alpha))=1,
\]
so \(\gamma=\alpha^L\) retains order \(>m\).

Now use the following collision recovery.

1. During baby construction, compute
   \[
   \gcd(\gamma^i-1,N),\qquad 1\le i<m.
   \]
   A proper gcd factors \(N\). If none occurs and the block has order \(>m\) modulo \(N\), both prime orders are at least \(m\).

2. Find exact giant–baby equalities modulo \(N\). An equality at index \(i\) proposes
   \[
   T=c+\ell^2(g_{\rm lo}+jm+i).
   \]
   Test \(T\) by solving
   \[
   Bz^2-Tz+AN=0
   \]
   and validating any divisor. For the true giant, uniqueness modulo \(p\) forces the matching index to be the true index.

3. Remove exact equalities and perform batched gcd collision detection. If a product gcd is \(N\), scan that giant against the \(m\) babies. Every nonunit difference now gives a proper factor, since exact equalities were removed. This scan happens at most once before success, so it adds only \(O(m)\) work.

This is the standard exact-equality/recovery principle behind Harvey’s collision machinery, applied to the shared cofactor handles. [Harvey, Algorithms 4.1–4.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2010.05450)

**The resulting order argument is deterministic on every semiprime; no small-order exceptional set and no one-sidedness estimate remain.** The almost-all ingredient is coverage.

For the consecutive-\(B\) construction, the large sieve gives
\[
\boxed{
\text{exceptional fraction}
\ll
\frac{(\log N)^2}{h}
\left(1+\frac{Y^2}{P}\right).
}
\]
Indeed, for fixed \(q\), uncovered integers \(p\) number
\[
\ll(P+Y^2)\frac{\log Y}{h}.
\]
Sum over prime \(q\)’s and divide by the \(\asymp N/(\log P\log Q)\) prime pairs in the band.

Here
\[
\frac{Y_{\rm coarse}^2}{P}
=N^{-1/6}R^{5/6}H^{-4/3},
\]
which equals \(H^{-4}\) at \(R=R_*\). Thus the coverage estimate remains strong throughout the useful DCL range. D′’s parameters also satisfy \(Y>h\) and \(Y^7>N\) there.

For \(H=N^{o(1)}\), the resulting per-band envelope is
\[
\boxed{
\min\left\{
N^{1/6}R^{1/6},\,
N^{1/4}R^{-1/4}
\right\}N^{o(1)}.
}
\]
At \(R=N^\rho\), its exponent is
\[
\begin{cases}
(1+\rho)/6,&0\le\rho\le1/5,\\
(1-\rho)/4,&1/5\le\rho\le1/3.
\end{cases}
\]
The maximum is \(1/5\), at \(\rho=1/5\). This improves extraction and simplifies the construction; it does not remove the ratio penalty.

**3. The curvature lemma is correct under explicit assumptions; its proposed universal interpretation is not.**

Let
\[
G(x)=\frac{B(r+Mx)+AN/(r+Mx)-c}{M^2}.
\]
For positive integer \(A\),
\[
G''(x)=\frac{2AN}{(r+Mx)^3}
\ge\frac{2R}{P}
\]
throughout the smaller-factor interval.

If \(G''\ge\kappa>0\), the preimage of a vertical interval of width \(m\) has at most two monotone components. Each has length at most \(\sqrt{2m/\kappa}\): integrate the second-derivative bound from the endpoint nearest the minimum. Therefore
\[
\boxed{
\#\{x\in\mathbb Z:G(x)\in[a,a+m]\}
\le2\sqrt{mP/R}+2.
}
\]
Your chain-rule cancellation of \(M^2\) is correct. This counts candidates in the residue class \(p=r+Mx\).

If \(G_{\rm count}\) separately charged windows must cover \(\asymp P\) candidates, then
\[
G_{\rm count}\left(2\sqrt{mP/R}+2\right)\gtrsim P.
\]
In the stated range, optimizing \(m+G_{\rm count}\) gives
\[
\boxed{
m+G_{\rm count}\gtrsim(PR)^{1/3}
=Q^{1/3}=N^{1/6}R^{1/6}.
}
\]
So the calculation is sound **for full-candidate coverage by individually charged consecutive exponent windows**.

The qualifications are substantial:

- **Coverage quantifier.** D′ covers the true factor for almost all pairs. It does not cover \(P\) candidates for each fixed input \(N\). Your lower-bound deduction inserts that stronger requirement.
- **Cost model.** Counting windows is not automatically a lower bound on computation. A future algorithm might process a structured family implicitly.
- **Intended versus accidental collisions.** The argument counts real-window coverage. It does not count useful collisions arising through reduction modulo a group order.
- **Allowed targets.** The inequality assumes a nonzero positive integer coefficient of \(N/p\). Zero coefficients, rational normalization, nonlinear handles and other representations require separate arguments.

For example, \(A=0,B=M\) gives \(T=Mp\), locked modulo \(M^2\) by \(p\bmod M\), and the normalized function is linear. This degenerates to an ordinary progression search; it supplies no faster algorithm, but disproves an unrestricted claim about “any linear-in-\(N\) target.”

The \(q\)-side does **not** escape the lemma within the positive-weight model. Its target is
\[
T'=Bq+Ap.
\]
Express it again in the smaller factor:
\[
T'(p)=Ap+BN/p.
\]
A residue class for \(q\bmod M\) corresponds bijectively to one for \(p\bmod M\), and
\[
\frac{d^2}{dx^2}\frac{T'(r+Mx)-c'}{M^2}
=\frac{2BN}{p^3}\ge\frac{2R}{P}.
\]
The coefficient roles swap, so the same bound applies.

My assessment of the suggested escape routes is:

| Modification | Concrete assessment |
|---|---|
| Composite locks | \(M^2\) still cancels from the second derivative. Multiple root classes must be counted. No improvement follows merely from making \(M\) composite. |
| Both-side extraction | Removes extraction failures, as above; it does not shrink these windows. |
| Rational weights | Clearing denominators restores integer coefficients and exponent scaling. Taking fractional group powers needs an additional justified operation. |
| Multipliers \(kN\) | Balancing by \(k\asymp R\) naturally incurs \((kN)^{1/6}\asymp N^{1/6}R^{1/6}\). This reproduces the penalty. |
| HH local lattices | With \(S=Mm_0\), their usual accounting is \(S+\sqrt N/S^{3/2}\). The nonzero-\(A\) regime has \(A=O(\sqrt{S/R})\); optimizing recovers \(N^{1/5}\), without a ratio improvement below the crossover. |
| McKee–Pinch pigeonhole | Guarantees suitable weighted congruences, but its proved accounting does not yield the desired \(1/6\) exponent. |
| Nonlinear \(E(N)\), higher-degree lattices, other groups | Outside this curvature proof. I found no rigorous construction attaining the requested uniform bound. They remain loopholes in an impossibility claim. |

The HH lattice window and coefficient bounds follow directly from their Proposition 3.3. [Harvey–Hittmeir 2022](https://arxiv.org/html/2105.11105)

Thus: the lemma explains the \(R^{1/6}\) penalty in this model. It does not establish that every deterministic or almost-all factoring algorithm must pay it.

**4. I found no published result supplying the missing uniform ratio theorem.**

The relevant literature distinguishes rigorous bounds from heuristic speedups:

| Work | Relevant result |
|---|---|
| [Lehman 1974](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1974-0340163-2) | Rational approximations to the factor ratio give a deterministic \(N^{1/3}\) algorithm; no intermediate-ratio sub-\(1/5\) theorem follows. |
| [McKee 1996, Euler method](https://doi.org/10.1112/blms/28.4.351) | Proved \(N^{1/3+\eta}\) algorithm. |
| [McKee 1999, speeding Fermat](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=010e639e5176f16cee1b8aff586141f4d5c8eb10&repid=rep1&type=pdf) | The \(N^{1/4+\eta}\) speedup is heuristic. |
| [McKee–Pinch 1996, §5](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-61581-4_57) | The weighted congruence gives cost \(\widetilde O(\max(\lambda p,\mu q)/k^2)\) per lock. Their pigeonhole construction proves \(N^{1/3+\eta}\); the balanced \(1/4\) variant is heuristic. I checked the repository’s scanned pages. |
| [Hittmeir 2020](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16729) | Proved deterministic \(N^{2/9+o(1)}\). |
| [Harvey 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.05450), [Harvey–Hittmeir 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11105) | General deterministic exponent \(1/5\); the latter improves logarithmic factors. |
| [Gao–Feng–Hu–Pan, 2512.19076](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.19076v1) | Their stated factoring theorem assumes \(p,q=\Theta(\sqrt N)\) and improves the logarithmic factor to \(\log^{13/5}N/(\log\log N)^{3/5}\). It supplies no polynomial-ratio extension. |
| [Harvey–Hittmeir 2026](https://arxiv.org/html/2601.11131) | Removes the lower restriction on the target order \(D\), enabling the extraction combination above. It supplies no ratio-dependent factoring exponent improvement by itself. |

Carella’s claimed \(1/6\) result has a concrete proof defect. In Theorem 5.1, write the correctly expanded polynomial as
\[
f(x,y)=(P_0+Dx)(Q_0+Dy)-N,
\qquad D\asymp N^{1/6}.
\]
Because \(P_0Q_0\equiv N\pmod D\), **every coefficient is divisible by \(D\)**. The displayed coefficient list omits the factors \(D\) on the linear terms and then asserts primitivity. After removing the content, at \(X=Y=N^{1/3}\),
\[
W\ll N/D=N^{5/6}.
\]
The cited bivariate condition would require
\[
XY<W^{2/3}\ll N^{5/9},
\]
whereas \(XY=N^{2/3}\). Hence that proof establishes neither the balanced claim nor its asserted unbalanced extension. [Carella, equations (17)–(21)](https://arxiv.org/html/1308.2891)

For \(R=N^\rho\), the concrete verdict is therefore:

- For \(0<\rho<1/5\), the derivation above gives an almost-all exponent \((1+\rho)/6<1/5\). I found no prior theorem covering every such prescribed semiprime band with the requested uniform \(1/6\) exponent.
- For \(1/5<\rho\le1/3\), Pollard–Strassen already gives the rigorous worst-case exponent \((1-\rho)/4<1/5\).
- At \(R=N^{1/5+o(1)}\), both meet at \(N^{1/5+o(1)}\). None of the checked results removes this peak.